WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.525
Building a long line of workstations?

00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:08.548
There is a smarter way than buying complete units side by side.

00:00:09.148 --> 00:00:12.950
If every station stands on its own, each one needs two uprights.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:18.020
A long line ends up with doubled uprights everywhere, costing money and legroom.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.242
A starter frame stands on its own, with uprights at both ends.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:27.393
An adder frame shares one upright with the frame beside it, so it needs only one more.

00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:33.331
Join a starter with one or more adders, and you get a long continuous run with fewer frames.

00:00:33.931 --> 00:00:39.869
One electrical components packer we worked with joined a starter and an adder for one long,

00:00:39.869 --> 00:00:42.741
uninterrupted packing top with more legroom.

00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:46.783
A shirt maker began with one starter and two adder flow racks,

00:00:46.783 --> 00:00:50.545
feeding a matching kitting workstation, ready to extend later.

00:00:51.145 --> 00:00:55.029
So plan your line in starters and adders, and grow it bay by bay.

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:57.249
How many stations does your line need?

00:00:57.529 --> 00:01:01.809
Comment below, and next, see Link Workstation versus Link Workstand.

00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:06.654
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

